&lt;td&gt;SciChart&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;SciChart WPF&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Billions of points per dataset&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;SciChart&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Figures, ours included, come from each vendor's own documentation and test setups. The Chart.js performance guide suggests decimating data before it reaches the chart and rendering in a web worker with OffscreenCanvas.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Many Charts on One Screen
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scientific dashboards often show many linked views at once. Plotly's documentation says you will generally fit 4 to 8 WebGL figures on one page before hitting browser context limits. A twelve-panel view needs a workaround such as virtual WebGL, which Plotly notes runs slower. SciChart.js avoids that limit because, by default, it &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/documentation/js/current/Performance%20Tips.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;uses a single shared WebGL context for all charts&lt;/a&gt;, and its &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/documentation/js/current/2d-charts/subcharts-api/subcharts-api-overview/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;SubCharts API&lt;/a&gt; draws large grids of charts in one pass. SciChart publishes a figure of 64+ charts refreshed in under one second.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Long Sessions and Memory
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instrument software often runs for hours or days. SciChart lists &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/why-scichart-diagnostics-lifesciences/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;continuous 24/7 operation and zero memory growth&lt;/a&gt; among its published performance metrics, so long analyses and overnight acquisitions do not degrade. In the browser, SciChart.js holds chart data in WebAssembly memory rather than JavaScript arrays: up to 4 GB in the standard build, and up to 16 GB in 64-bit WebAssembly (wasm64) mode. The same rendering technology now supports &lt;a href="https://www.scichart.com/why-scichart-best-for-motorsport-and-automotive/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;most Formula One telemetry&lt;/a&gt;, simulation and engineering workflows, directly or indirectly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Lightweight Enough for Inexpensive Hardware
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Efficient rendering also lowers hardware requirements. That matters when an instrument ships with an embedded PC, or when field teams use low-cost tablets. PerkinElmer found SciChart's lower hardware requirements kept production costs down across its chromatography product range (see the case studies below).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Performance With Real Features Switched on
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The most useful benchmark is one that turns on the features your workflow needs. SciChart's &lt;a href="https://github.com/abtsoftware/javascript-chart-performance-test-suite" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;open-source JavaScript chart performance test suite&lt;/a&gt; compares libraries across scenarios such as multi-series and multi-axis charts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How Do You Choose a Cross-Platform Charting Library for JavaScript, WPF, and Mobile Lab Software?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Start with where each product in your suite runs, then pick a cross-platform charting library that covers all of it with the same API. For many instrument makers that means a desktop application at the instrument plus a browser view for review, with mobile for field or portable work.&lt;/p&gt;

